---
import Doc from "../../layouts/Doc.astro";
import scans from "../../data/scans.json";
import { AXES, EVIDENCE_CAP } from "../../lib/methodology";

// A real slug, so every example URL on this page resolves.
const example = scans[0]?.slug ?? "";
const BASE = "https://mcpwatchman.com";
const axisKeys = AXES.map((a) => `"${a.key}"`).join(", ");

const historyShape = `{
  "slug": "${example}",
  "name": "…",
  "points": [
    {
      "date": "2026-10-29",
      "scanned_at": "2026-10-29T03:28:02+00:00",
      "methodology_version": "0.2.0",
      "ruleset_version": "0.1.2",
      "scanner_version": "0.38.28",
      "axes": {
        "code_safety": { "score": 72, "assessed_weight": "1", "fault": null },
        "auth_posture": { "score": null, "assessed_weight": "0", "fault": "publisher" }
      }
    }
  ]
}`;
---

<Doc
  title="API reference — mcpwatchman"
  description="mcpwatchman's JSON API: static, unauthenticated endpoints for every published server's per-axis scores, coverage and evidence, and how to read them."
  canonical="https://mcpwatchman.com/api/"
  heading="API reference"
>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    <p>
      Everything on the site is also JSON. The API is a set of static files,
      regenerated after each nightly scan: no key, no account, and the same
      data the pages are built from.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="rules-heading">
    <h2 id="rules-heading">Before you read a score</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>There is no composite.</strong> Every response carries{" "}
        <code>"composite_published": false</code>. The weighted overall score
        is computed and withheld until calibration; do not derive one and
        present it as ours.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Read <code>assessed_weight</code> with every score.</strong> It
        is the share of the axis that was measured, a decimal string from{" "}
        <code>"0"</code> to <code>"1"</code>, rounded down. A score of 80 at{" "}
        <code>"0.25"</code> is 80 of a quarter of the axis, and must not be
        shown as 80.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>null</code> is not zero.</strong> A <code>null</code>{" "}
        score means not assessed; <code>fault</code> then says whose gap it is
        (<code>"publisher"</code> or <code>"project"</code>). See{" "}
        <a href="/methodology/">the methodology</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The set is pinned and only grows.</strong> A server's URLs never
        change once published. The set is not a random sample of the registry,
        and a server's absence is never a judgement.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Every response repeats these rules in its own <code>*_note</code>{" "}
      fields, so a record read on its own still carries them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="paper doc-paper" aria-labelledby="endpoints-heading">
    <h2 id="endpoints-heading">Endpoints</h2>
    <p>
      All under <code>{BASE}</code>, all <code>GET</code>, all{" "}
      <code>application/json</code>. Axis keys are {axisKeys}. No key and no
      cookie: every response is public, so a page on any origin may call it
      from a browser (<code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *</code>).
    </p>

    <h3><code>/api/index.json</code></h3>
    <p>
      Every published server, without evidence: name, slug, version, package,
      repository URL, <code>registry_state</code>, <code>scanned_at</code>,
      page and record URLs, and for each axis its <code>score</code>,{" "}
      <code>assessed_weight</code> and <code>fault</code>. Use it to find a
      server by package name or repository URL.
    </p>
    <pre><code>curl {BASE}/api/index.json</code></pre>

    <h3><code>/api/servers/&lt;slug&gt;.json</code></h3>
    <p>
      One server's full record — the machine twin of{" "}
      <code>/servers/&lt;slug&gt;/</code> — wrapped with the reading notes and
      its page URL. The slug is the registry name lowercased, every run of
      characters other than letters and digits replaced by one hyphen, with
      no hyphen at either end. Prefer this for a single server.
    </p>
    <pre><code>curl {BASE}/api/servers/{example}.json</code></pre>

    <h3><code>/api/servers.json</code></h3>
    <p>
      Every full record in one document, several megabytes. Prefer it to
      scraping pages when you need everything.
    </p>

    <h3><code>/api/servers/&lt;slug&gt;/history.json</code></h3>
    <p>
      One point per recorded scan of the server, with the versions that
      produced it and each axis's score, coverage and fault. The shape, with
      illustrative values:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{historyShape}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Points scored under different methodology or ruleset versions are not
      directly comparable; the version fields are there so you can tell.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="paper doc-paper" aria-labelledby="fields-heading">
    <h2 id="fields-heading">A server record</h2>
    <p>
      The fields of <code>server</code> in a per-server response. Notes in the
      response itself cover the subtler ones in more detail.
    </p>
    <dl class="defs">
      <div><dt>name, slug, version</dt><dd>The registry name and version scanned, and the URL-safe slug.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>scanned_at</dt><dd>When the scan ran, ISO 8601 with offset.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>methodology_version, ruleset_version, scanner_version</dt><dd>What produced the scores. <code>ruleset_version</code> is empty when no rule ran.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>repository_url, repository_commit, package</dt><dd>What was read. The commit names the exact tree; <code>null</code> when no repository was read.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>source_state, source_subject, source_reason</dt><dd>Whether source could be read, and if not, whether the repository or the package was the problem, in a sentence.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>ref_matched_version</dt><dd><code>true</code>, <code>false</code> (the default branch was read instead of the release) or <code>null</code> (nothing established it). <code>null</code> is not <code>false</code>.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>registry_state, registry_note</dt><dd><code>"listed"</code> with an empty note, or another value that explains itself in the note: <code>"deprecated"</code>, <code>"delisted"</code>, <code>"stale"</code>.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>axes.&lt;key&gt;.score</dt><dd>0–100, or <code>null</code> when not assessed.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>axes.&lt;key&gt;.assessed_weight</dt><dd>The measured share of the axis, a decimal string.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>axes.&lt;key&gt;.fault</dt><dd>Whose gap an unassessed axis is; <code>null</code> on a scored axis.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>axes.&lt;key&gt;.unmeasured_faults</dt><dd>On a scored axis, whose gaps the unmeasured remainder is. Empty when measured in full.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>axes.&lt;key&gt;.reason</dt><dd>Why the axis is unassessed or partial, in a sentence.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>axes.&lt;key&gt;.evidence</dt><dd>The trail: label, detail, path, line, excerpt, url, and for a finding its rule or advisory ID, severity, <code>first_seen</code> and whether it <code>deducts</code>. At most {EVIDENCE_CAP} per axis, most severe first.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>axes.&lt;key&gt;.evidence_omitted</dt><dd>How many further findings were not listed. The score counts them all.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="other-heading">
    <h2 id="other-heading">Other machine surfaces</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/feed/high-severity.xml">/feed/high-severity.xml</a> — Atom: critical and high findings new on a tracked page.</li>
      <li><a href="/llms.txt">/llms.txt</a> — a plain-text guide to the site for agents.</li>
      <li><code>/badge/&lt;slug&gt;.svg</code> — the README badge, which names a server's lowest-scoring axis.</li>
      <li><a href="/cli/">The CLI</a> reads these same files.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A hosted, queryable API with on-demand scans is specified but not built.
      Until it is, these files are the API.
    </p>
  </section>
</Doc>
